<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
<p class="font-bold">For a fixed pair of envelopes, the gain and loss cancel.</p>
<table class="w-full text-base"><thead><tr class="border-b-2 border-ink"><th class="p-2 text-left">You first pick</th><th class="p-2">The other envelope</th><th class="p-2 text-right">Gain if you swap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-b border-ink"><td class="p-2">£10</td><td class="p-2">£20</td><td class="p-2 text-right">+£10</td></tr><tr><td class="p-2">£20</td><td class="p-2">£10</td><td class="p-2 text-right">−£10</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-base"><div class="border border-ink p-3"><strong>Half the time</strong><p>You gain £10.</p></div><div class="border border-ink p-3"><strong>Half the time</strong><p>You lose £10.</p></div></div>
<figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">Two equally likely choices give an average switching gain of £0. The tempting argument instead says that after seeing any amount, double and half are equally likely. That needs a probability rule for how the money was put into the envelopes, and cannot be true for every possible amount.</figcaption></figure>